1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,090
Welcome to our deep dive into government enterprise architecture.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:13,180
Uh, today we're looking through a stack of research papers, government IT manuals, and our own notes to really figure out why public sector IT teams waste

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:18,860
months, I mean, sometimes a whole year, just drafting foundational rules from scratch.

4
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:28,580
Yeah, it's a huge problem. We're also gonna look at the PAERA framework, so that's the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

5
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:38,360
We wanna see how IT teams can transform these principles from, you know, basically useless wall decorations into powerful working tools that settle design arguments before they even start.

6
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:42,000
Right. 'Cause every architecture team falls into this initial drafting trap, don't they?

7
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:43,580
Oh, absolutely. Every single time.

8
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:51,720
Like, someone decides the very first step to modernizing government tech is writing down a list of core principles, and then three months later you have, what, 40 contradictory drafts?

9
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,820
Yeah, 40 drafts and a completely stalled project.

10
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,900
That blank page is incredibly dangerous.

11
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:57,980
So how do we fix it?

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,800
Well, the solution is to adopt rather than draft.

13
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:07,340
PAERA provides 10 baseline principles that have already been globally debated and refined.

14
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:11,160
Wait, so instead of arguing over definitions, you just inherit the foundational thinking.

15
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,060
Exactly. You inherit the thinking and save a year of wasted effort.

16
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:19,760
I mean, I do wonder if inheriting 10 pre-written rules acts like a...

17
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:21,100
Well, like a cookie cutter trap.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:22,430
How do you mean?

19
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,060
Because government environments are so unique, right?

20
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:31,920
Forcing a generic framework onto a specific country's systems just seems like a recipe for friction.

21
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:35,520
I get that, but you aren't forcing a final product onto the system.

22
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:38,380
You're starting with a universally accepted baseline.

23
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:43,020
So for example, one of the 10 PAERA principles is the once only rule.

24
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:47,120
Oh, meaning you ask a citizen for their information, like their address or tax ID.

25
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:48,580
Right. Exactly one time.

26
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:49,850
Right. Exactly one time.

27
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:54,560
And then the systems share that data in the background- Yeah... rather than making them fill out five different forms.

28
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,480
Precisely. It fundamentally shifts the burden from the citizen back to the government.

29
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:06,270
Another baseline rule is interoperability by default, which just ensures systems can actually talk to each other.

30
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:08,220
So these aren't restrictive constraints.

31
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:14,300
No, not at all. They are the universal ground rules every public sector architecture actually needs to function efficiently.

32
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:21,880
Okay, but since we're not starting from scratch, we need a way to make those baseline principles fit a specific local environment.

33
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:23,560
Yeah, that's where tailoring comes in.

34
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:26,540
It looks a lot like buying a high quality tailored suit.

35
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:31,960
You know, you don't weave the fabric yourself, you just take excellent material, the PAERA principles.

36
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:33,500
Yeah. And localize the fit.

37
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:37,560
That's a great way to put it. And the tailoring process actually happens in three steps.

38
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:41,240
First, you take those 10 core principles as your baseline.

39
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:46,360
Right. Second, you point each principle at your country's specific legislation.

40
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,680
So like local data protection acts or procurement rules.

41
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:51,900
Exactly, to give them real legal teeth.

42
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:53,170
But what if a country

43
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:58,740
genuinely requires something totally unique that isn't covered by the 10 baselines?

44
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,620
There has to be room to add custom rules, surely.

45
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:05,620
Well, that is step three, but there's a major catch.

46
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:12,260
You can add a new principle, but you must formally document the exact reason why it's necessary.

47
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:18,450
Ah. So you can't just throw in a new rule because some software vendor suggested it in a meeting.

48
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:22,840
Exactly. Tailoring the principles to local laws makes them relevant.

49
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:28,100
But to prevent them from becoming empty slogans, they have to actually change how decisions are made.

50
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:30,680
Right, because a real principle actually settles an argument.

51
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,860
Yes. And to do that, I'm gathering it needs a rigid structure.

52
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:39,500
Right. Like a statement of what we are doing, a reason why, and a hard implication.

53
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:43,870
Yes. And the implication is what separates a real principle from just a catchy slogan.

54
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,880
Let's look at the principle of reuse before build.

55
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:51,340
Okay, so the statement for that would be simple: we consume an existing building block before building a new one.

56
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:53,970
Right. And the reason is totally financial.

57
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:57,260
The country pays for the tech once, not many times over.

58
00:03:57,450 --> 00:03:59,430
And the implication is where the authority comes in.

59
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:07,640
Exactly. So if a team proposes building its own custom identity verification system, they must first prove to the enterprise architecture board

60
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:12,180
why the shared existing identity system fails to meet their needs.

61
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:17,720
Wow. So without that written implication, the board basically has no authority to say no.

62
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:21,300
Right. A bad design just gets approved because there's no mechanism to stop it.

63
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:26,640
If a principle never forces a change or refuses a bad design, it is entirely useless.

64
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:29,580
That makes total sense. So to sum this up for you listening,

65
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,420
public IT teams should never spend their first year writing principles from scratch.

66
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:44,250
Definitely not. Just adopt PAERA's 10 baseline rules, tailor them directly to your local laws, and attach written implications so they can settle real architectural

67
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:44,730
arguments.

68
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:53,600
Doing this ensures you make foundational decisions once, freeing up your team to focus on actually building systems rather than endlessly arguing over the rules.

